      "text": "To determine the area of Mateen's rectangular backyard, we need to find the dimensions of the rectangle given the information about the number of times he needs to walk to cover 1000 meters.\n\nFirst, let's denote the length of the rectangular backyard by \\( l \\) meters and the width by \\( w \\) meters.\n\nAccording to the problem, it takes Mateen 25 times to walk the length 25 times. This means that the length \\( l \\) must be 1000 meters:\n\\[\n25l = 1000 \\implies l = \\frac{1000}{25} = 40 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nNext, it takes Mateen 10 times to walk the perimeter 10 times. The perimeter \\( P \\) of a rectangle is given by:\n\\[\nP = 2l + 2w\n\\]\nSince it takes 10 times to walk the perimeter 10 times, we have:\n\\[\n10 \\times 2l = 1000 \\implies 2l = 100 \\implies l = 50 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\nThis seems contradictory with our previous calculation. Let's correct this by re-evaluating the information given. The correct interpretation should be that it takes Mateen 10 times to walk the perimeter 10 times, which means:\n\\[\n10 \\times (2l + 2w) = 1000 \\implies 2l + 2w = 100 \\implies l + w = 50\n\\]\nWe already know \\( l = 40 \\), so substituting \\( l \\) into the equation:\n\\[\n40 + w = 50 \\implies w = 10 \\text{ meters}\n\\]\n\nNow we have the dimensions of the rectangle: length \\( l = 40 \\) meters and width \\( w = 10 \\) meters. The area \\( A \\) of the rectangle is given by:\n\\[\nA = l \\times w = 40 \\times 10 = 400 \\text{ square meters}\n\\]\n\nThus, the area of Mateen's backyard is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{400}\n\\]",
